	Firewalls around the EC2 instances:
		Introduction to Security Groups:
			Security groups are the fundamental of network security in AWS cloud.
			Control how traffic is allowed into and out of our EC2 instances.
			Contains only 'allow' rules
			
			www (inbound traffic) --> |  [Security Group]   |--> EC2 instance
			    (outbound traffic)<-- |			|--  	
	
	
	
			
	Security Groups Deeper Dive:
		
		Security groups are acting as a "firewall" on EC2 instances.
		So, only the authorized IP address can access our instances and only the authorized IP address can receive our sent data from the instance.
		It is a many to many relation and an instance can have multiple SGs and One SG can be attached to multiple instances.
		These are locked down to a region/VPC combination.
		Lives outside the EC2 instance and if the traffic is blocked the EC2 instance wont see it.
		Good to maintain one separate SG for SSH access.
		If your application is not accessible(time out), then its a SG issue.
		But if the application gives you a "Connection Refused" error, then it is an application error or its not launched.
		All 'Inbound' Traffic is blocked by default.
		All 'Outbound' Traffic is authorized by default.
		
		Communicating other security groups:
		
		When an EC2 instance(or any other instance) has a security group attached to it, we can setup an inbound rule to allow other 
		SGs to communicate with 1st EC2. This concept comes under load balancers.
		
		
